// The Hash/Hasher shape with its three roles in three packages, as a
// running program: `hashkit` owns the traits (`Hash::hash` carrying its
// own bounded generic, plus a byte-splitting bounded generic standing in
// for a default method body); `hashables` implements them for local types
// — Bernstein's djb2 and the hashable types (a ground `Point` impl and a
// generic `Pair` impl recursing through both bounds), each in its own
// module; and the app is a pure *user* — it implements nothing, names the
// extern traits only in bounds, and dispatches through impls it never
// wrote. The dependency graph is a diamond (app -> hashkit and app ->
// hashables -> hashkit), so the trait items load both directly and via
// the implementer's re-shipped interface — exercising idempotent trait
// reload — while the app instantiates `hashables`' generic impl method
// and `hashkit`'s shipped generic bodies at its own call shapes
// (weak_odr), with the ground methods link-resolved from the archives.
// The printed values are the real djb2 digests of the little-endian byte
// streams, pinned against an independent reference computation.

// RUN: %rene build --manifest-path %S/trait_hash/rene.ncl --build-dir %t.bdir %rrc_linker | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LISTING

// LISTING: {{[/\\]}}dev{{[/\\]}}hashdemo

// RUN: %t.bdir/dev/hashdemo%exe_ext | %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=OUT

// djb2 over LE(1) ++ LE(2)…
// OUT: 7944528385982877352
// …over LE(3) ++ LE(4)…
// OUT-NEXT: 445791163684897836
// …and Pair{Point(3,4), Point(5,6)} folds the same state onward.
// OUT-NEXT: 5582476884098275031
